Steps to be Performed:

1- Clone the React Application from GitHub
2- Install dependencies
3- Create your own GitHub repository
4- Push the Code to your new repository
5- Deploy the application on AWS Amplify

#1 Clone the React Application from GitHub:
Open a terminal and clone the repository to your local machine using the following command:

git clone https://github.com/techwithlucy/ztc-projects-intermediate.git

Navigate to the project directory:

cd projects
cd intermediate
cd project4

#2 Install Dependencies:
Install the required dependencies for the React app by running the below commands in the terminal:
python
npm install
npm start
Check if the React application is running locally by checking: http://localhost:3000 on the browser. 

#3 Create Your Own GitHub Repository:

Log in to GitHub and create a new repository.
Provide a repository name, such as serverless-cloud-dictionary.
(Optional) Add a description, such as "Serverless Cloud Dictionary Application Frontend."
Do not initialize the repository with a README or .gitignore.
Click Create repository.
﻿
#4 Push the Code to Your New Repository:
In the project directory, set up Git to push your local code to the new GitHub repository:

git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git remote remove origin
git remote add origin <your-new-repository-url>
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main
git init: Initializes a Git repository in the project folder.
git add .: Stages all project files for commit.
git commit -m "Initial commit": Commits the staged files.
git remote add origin <your-new-repository-url>: #Links the local repository to your new GitHub repository.
git push -u origin main: Pushes the code to the main branch of the new repository.

#5 Deploy the Application to AWS Amplify:

Log in to the AWS Management Console and navigate to AWS Amplify.
Click on Deploy an App.
Select GitHub as the source and authenticate if prompted.
Choose your new repository and the branch (usually main).
AWS Amplify will automatically detect the React application and configure the build settings. Review and confirm the configuration, then click Save and Deploy.
Wait for Amplify to complete the build and deployment process.
After deployment, Amplify will provide a URL (e.g., https://your-app-name.amplifyapp.com).
Open the URL in a browser to confirm the React application is live and functioning.

This completes the first step of setting up the frontend and hosting it on AWS Amplify.
